      <section>
        <div class="sec-kicker">Why KeronisEngine</div>
        <h2>Built for predictive models, not just rule-based scripts</h2>
        <p class="sec-desc">
          NautilusTrader is a strong general-purpose platform for Python strategies.
          KeronisEngine is purpose-built when predictive models and sub-millisecond execution sit in the hot path.
        </p>
        <div class="diff-grid">
          <div class="diff-card accent">
            <h3>KeronisEngine</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Eliminates feature-code drift:</strong> The same compiled C++ feature code generates offline Parquet training sets and serves live inference.</li>
              <li><strong>Sub-millisecond ONNX in-process:</strong> Score gradient boosters or neural nets directly on tick arrival without IPC or Python GIL overhead.</li>
              <li><strong>Multi-asset &amp; prediction-market native:</strong> Trade crypto, Polymarket digital options, and US stocks (IBKR) out of the box—and extend cleanly to sports betting or new venues.</li>
              <li><strong>Complete live stack included:</strong> Ships with the C++ engine, backtester, realistic paper venue, risk/staleness controls, and the Python CCXT/IBKR/venue proxy.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-card">
            <h3>When to pick NautilusTrader or HftBacktest</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Pick NautilusTrader</strong> if you prefer writing strategies in Python and want a large ecosystem of Python-native broker adapters.</li>
              <li><strong>Pick HftBacktest</strong> if your primary research depends on strict L3 order-queue-position simulation on CEX order books in Python/Numba.</li>
              <li><strong>Deep internals:</strong> Want the low-level threading, book layout, and crash-recovery design? Read the <a href="/architecture/" style="color: var(--cyan); text-decoration: underline;">Architecture docs</a>.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

